51
00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:42.020
Simon N Sponberg: Now that people have had that, let's do the introduction. Okay, you all have had a lot of workshops at this point in time. I don't need to tell you that muscle is special, and muscle is special in very particular ways, but one of the ways that it's really, really important is that it's fairly unique as a tissue.

52
00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.520
Simon N Sponberg: Because it's so hierarchical.

53
00:14:44.520 --> 00:15:07.829
Simon N Sponberg: And so well organized. And it has certain kinds of things that are widely conserved across species. And one of those is the sort of fundamental structure of skeletal muscle, at least, although it's true for cardiac, too, that it's organized into these hierarchical scale… length scales that are typically organized around this sarcomere.

54
00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:19.450
Simon N Sponberg: And one of the cool things about sarcomeres is sarcomeres is regardless of whether you're a human, a cockroach, or a blue whale, they tend to all be roughly the same length, especially if you're a vertebrate.

55
00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:25.619
Simon N Sponberg: If you're a vertebrate, your sarcomeres vary by very little, usually no more than 1 to 10 microns.

56
00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:42.800
Simon N Sponberg: And, that even extends to most invertebrates. And so that's pretty remarkable. You can have many, many ranges of size, but you have this almost what we would call in physics, an invariant length scale that is, present throughout those. And it sort of tells you that there's a lot of conserved organization of muscle.

57
00:15:42.810 --> 00:16:07.430
Simon N Sponberg: So even if you get a really big muscle, at this level, you're going to get a lot of conservation of the fundamental structure. And many of you know already, or should remember, that that's because of the structure of the molecular filaments and proteins that are enabling muscle to contract. This interdigitation of thick filaments and thin filaments, where the thick filaments contain the myosin that have the little active heads.

58
00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:19.500
Simon N Sponberg: illustrated here on the bottom right, and the thin filaments have the binding sites for those. And again, we're just, as a reminder, we call when a bias and head binds to an actin-containing thin filament, we call that a cross bridge.

59
00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:28.580
Simon N Sponberg: Okay, so there's been some discussion about this. This molecular scale is really essential, though, because it's at this scale that it's oftentimes really hard to get good information.

60
00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:48.040
Simon N Sponberg: And that's what we're going to address a little bit today, and it's especially hard to get good information dynamically. What that means is when you want to make a video. And so you… many of you have probably seen a video like this online of a myosin head binding forming a cross bridge and sliding an actin filament along. And these videos are great for visualization.

61
00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:58.009
Simon N Sponberg: But you better be pretty dang critical of these videos. There's a lot of things that this misses. First of all, like, ATP just sort of floats in from the ether there. There's nothing else

62
00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:04.069
Simon N Sponberg: drawn in this picture, and the length scale here is about 20 nanometers, okay?

63
00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:19.120
Simon N Sponberg: A nanometer is the size of a water molecule between an angstrom and a nanometer, so there's, like, a very finite amount of space here, and even the individual component clutter of other ions in this space are going to make it very, very crowded.

64
00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:25.450
Simon N Sponberg: So, there's a lot of things that these animations miss, and there's a lot of assumptions that they make.

65
00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:42.410
Simon N Sponberg: So, for example, if you watch the video again, the actin filament slides left and right. And in fact, if we think about the sliding filament theory, that's what we've talked about for a long time, is that we always sort of draw it as these thick filaments and these thin filaments sliding across each other.

66
00:17:43.760 --> 00:18:02.140
Simon N Sponberg: But I just got done telling you that muscle is really structured hierarchically and within a lot of organization. And really, actually, muscle makes this 3D pattern, and this is going to become really important. So, here's a fundamental question that that animation didn't even get at yet.

67
00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:08.999
Simon N Sponberg: If you slide this way, Horizontally. What happens, in this case, vertically?

68
00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:10.890
Simon N Sponberg: Right?

69
00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:23.750
Simon N Sponberg: So most things, when you push them, they expand, right? If they're isovolumetric, they expand. And so that actually goes back to a really, really important study that was done a long, long time ago by this guy, Jan Swammerdam.

70
00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:33.320
Simon N Sponberg: In 1666, he was a really good experimentalist, and he was a big devotee of Descartes, the guy who came up with Cartesian coordinates, right?

71
00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:46.460
Simon N Sponberg: And Descartes had this idea of vital energies, that what happens when a muscle contracts is that there's this vital energy taken from the ether that is pumped through the nerves, and it swells the muscle up.

72
00:18:46.660 --> 00:19:10.360
Simon N Sponberg: Right? And so Swamadam wanted to test this. He wanted to measure the volume of this spirit energy, of this ether. And so what he knew… what he did is he took a frog muscle, he put it inside a chamber here on the right, and he put… filled it with fluid, and he put a little float at the top, and they… and then he took the nerve, and he stretched it out inside of the chamber. And they knew that if you tweaked the nerve, and the muscle was fresh, you could get the muscle to contract.

73
00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:19.739
Simon N Sponberg: And so his idea here is that he would pinch the nerve, and the muscle would contract, and the float would go up, and the amount that the float goes up tells you how much of the volume of that spirit energy is.

74
00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:27.710
Simon N Sponberg: And of course, he did this experiment, and the float didn't move, because muscles are isovolumetric, at least at the cell scale.

75
00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:48.480
Simon N Sponberg: And this was actually really problematic for Swammerdam, because he wrote in his journal that, you know, there must be something wrong with my experiment, because there's no way Desart is wrong, which is a good lesson, never trust your advisors. In fact, that's the first rule of my lab. And in fact, it has sort of a sad story, because, like, after this experiment, Jan Swamderdam joined an end-of-days cult.

76
00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:55.770
Simon N Sponberg: Where he eventually died. So, like, he actually left science because of this. So trust your data, not your advisor.

77
00:19:56.260 --> 00:20:01.629
Simon N Sponberg: But please do listen to your advisor. Okay. Muscle is isovolumetric at the cell scale.

78
00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:08.480
Simon N Sponberg: But what about at that myofilament lettuce scale? What about at the sarcomere level? What happens if you shorten a muscle.

79
00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:11.230
Simon N Sponberg: Does the lattice expand?

80
00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:13.070
Simon N Sponberg: Or does it shrink?

81
00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:33.130
Simon N Sponberg: And this is really important, because if you think about what's happening there, you've got a myosin head interacting with actin. There's a binding site on actin. When you have two molecules interacting, you can draw, like, a free energy diagram. This is just sort of a cartoon version of it, but it is sort of this shape. And so, like, the further this actin filament is away from the myosin.

82
00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:38.600
Simon N Sponberg: The less likely it's going to be to bind, or the more likely it's going to be to bind, depending on where it is.

83
00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:55.659
Simon N Sponberg: In that space. So if the lattice is moving, it's going to affect the binding probability, and in fact, we know that there's significant forces produced by the myosin heads, as well as in the axial direction, as well as in the radial direction, as well as in the axial direction.

84
00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.609
Simon N Sponberg: So, what is the role of this radial change in lattice spacing?

85
00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:09.270
Simon N Sponberg: for the function of a muscle. And how the heck would you measure this? Because it's at the time… at the length scale of tens of nanometers.

86
00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:16.880
Simon N Sponberg: And tens of nanometers is shorter than the wavelength of visible light, so you're pretty much screwed if you want to try and measure it with a light microscope.

87
00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:30.350
Simon N Sponberg: Right? You can use an electron microscope, but then you have to freeze the muscle, fracture it, and measure it statically. So what if you want to do it dynamically? Well, you can do what any sane person does, which is you take your muscle and you bring it to a particle accelerator.

88
00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:54.409
Simon N Sponberg: This is the advanced photon source at Argonne National Lab, and the MZ collaborative team includes Tom Irving and his team there, Wei Kong Ma, at the BioCat X-ray beamline. And so what, if you haven't seen a particle accelerator before, there's a linear accelerator that spins up a bunch of electrons, and then you strip off photons from that, and the photons are extremely high energy, they're X-rays.

89
00:21:54.500 --> 00:22:13.120
Simon N Sponberg: But you'll get x-rays on the dental office. It's not so hard to create x-rays. What's really impressive about these particle accelerators is the amount of photons they can produce. They can create a really, really bright x-ray beam, and then you can focus it down, and you can shoot it through muscle. And when you shoot it through living muscle.

90
00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:20.610
Simon N Sponberg: you see something really interesting. You see a pattern like this, and this is what we're going to talk about today. This is a diffraction pattern.

91
00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:42.320
Simon N Sponberg: And Jiangkun will introduce what a diffraction pattern is and why it happens. But for now, what it is, is it's going to correspond to the spacing differences between the thick filaments and the thin filaments. So if you look at one of those sarcomeres in cross-section, the red thick filaments and the blue thin filaments are organized in this regular lattice. Well, a lattice is a crystal.

92
00:22:42.320 --> 00:23:05.839
Simon N Sponberg: And when you shoot energy light through a crystal at the right wavelength, it will diffract, it will create these little spots, and those spots will help correspond to the spacings that you see in that lattice. So if we want to tell if the muscle is changing the distance between actin and myosin, we can use this X-ray diffraction technique. There's a lot of other things it can tell you, and Jiangkun will talk about that.

93
00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:12.719
Simon N Sponberg: shortly. But I want to give you one example of how we use this, and it was to resolve… there's other diffraction peaks I'll also talk about.

94
00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:36.819
Simon N Sponberg: We can also do this with really high time resolution with modern X-ray detectors, because we have so many photons going through. Here is it shooting through a flight muscle and a moth, but you can get samples down to the level of a millisecond. So you can potentially image the diffraction pattern and get information about the contraction of muscles, and this is at the molecular scale. And so here is a much dimmer image, so you only see three spots of that diffraction pattern.

95
00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:47.809
Simon N Sponberg: But this is actually a cockroach muscle in the beam that's contracting in about 40 milliseconds. It's slowed down, and we're watching the change in those dots. The rotation is just because of a change in the angle of the muscle.

96
00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:59.340
Simon N Sponberg: But the change in the spot's intensity and their spacing, if you look carefully, will help correspond to the change in the spacing of the myofilament lattice with inside muscle.

97
00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:16.590
Simon N Sponberg: All right. So, can these microscopic lattice spacing properties, nanoscale properties, affect the macroscopic properties of muscle as a whole tissue? That's the first thing you'd want to know, right, for us in IMSI. And so, let me start you off with everybody's favorite animal, a cockroach.

98
00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:30.499
Simon N Sponberg: I'm sorry, I should have given a trigger warning before that ran across the screen. The cockroach is very interesting, you've probably heard a few different examples of this before, because it's been very well studied, a couple of its muscles, and a cockroach has a mystery embedded in its leg.

99
00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:49.429
Simon N Sponberg: And this mystery was explored by Travis, too. It was first identified in Anna Ann's classic papers from 2000, which I really encourage everybody to read if they're part of MZ. But Travis wanted to figure out this mystery, and here's the mystery. There are two muscles, there are many muscles in the leg of the cockroach, but there's two of them. Muscle 178, 179, don't worry about the numbers.

100
00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:54.029
Simon N Sponberg: The red muscle and the blue muscle is fun. These two muscles are really interesting, because they act on the same joint.

101
00:24:54.290 --> 00:25:06.320
Simon N Sponberg: They have the exact same neural activation, the same single motor neuron goes to both of these muscles. They are effectively together a single motor unit, and there is only one motor neuron that goes into it. Invertebrates are weird.

102
00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:09.730
Simon N Sponberg: They have the same mass and the same moment arm.

103
00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:17.989
Simon N Sponberg: They have the same macroscopic organization, meaning, like, the panacea angle and everything like that. And they have the same force length and force velocity curves.

104
00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:19.320
Simon N Sponberg: Pretty much.

105
00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:25.340
Simon N Sponberg: Okay? So everything we characterize a muscle with says these two muscles are exactly the same.

106
00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:29.480
Simon N Sponberg: But then you do the work loops.

107
00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:36.969
Simon N Sponberg: And you've had work loops. Here's the work loops for the two muscle. One is a break, and one is bifunctional, and actually produces net positive work.

108
00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:42.229
Simon N Sponberg: So, one's entirely dissipating energy, and one is producing a large amount of positive work.

109
00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:45.690
Simon N Sponberg: Why? What's different about these two things?

110
00:25:45.690 --> 00:26:00.180
Simon N Sponberg: That's what Travis wanted to discuss. And what he hypothesized is that this difference between a brake and a motor-like muscle is maybe there's something different about the molecular spacing. But if that's true, it can only be present during the dynamic conditions of that muscle contracting.

111
00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:06.359
Simon N Sponberg: Because the steady-state properties, the length… force-velocity curve and the force-length curve, have to be the same.

112
00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:11.199
Simon N Sponberg: So, he took cockroaches to Argon, and he shot x-rays

113
00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:36.120
Simon N Sponberg: the particle accelerator through them, and he measured the lattice spacing. And John Kuhn will talk a little bit more about how we do this, but one of these dots, the 10 dot, corresponds to the lattice spacing between the thick filaments, between the myosin planes, and he'll talk a little bit about why this is the case in just a minute. But for now, I want you to know that you can make these measurements, and I want you to tell you about the kinds of things you can do with those measurements. So by measuring the distance of that

114
00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:46.879
Simon N Sponberg: first peak in the diffraction muscle, one from the motor, one's from the brake up there. We can tell how… if there's a difference in the spacing at the level of a few nanometers.

115
00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:57.530
Simon N Sponberg: And here's what he did. He measured the lattice spacing as a function of stretching the muscle to different strains, and just holding it at those different lengths. And what he noticed was something really interesting.

116
00:26:57.530 --> 00:27:18.530
Simon N Sponberg: Muscle 178 has a slightly tighter lattice. At all lengths, it's basically… before it's activated, the muscle is 1 nanometer tighter in its lattice spacing change. This is only a 2% difference, but it's a really important thing because a 1 nanometer difference in spacing on a free energy landscape is a big difference in binding probability.

117
00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:28.139
Simon N Sponberg: And here's the cool thing, he then activated these two muscles like you would for a force-length curve, right? He did isometric activation experiments.

118
00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:32.290
Simon N Sponberg: And he activated them, and an interesting thing happened.

119
00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:50.889
Simon N Sponberg: The muscle on the left, I like to call it the morning… it's not a morning person. It has to get up and stretch. It stretches out the lattice spacing, and it actually increases its lattice spacing as it's activated. Whereas the one on the right is a morning person. It's ready to go. It's… as soon as you activate, the lattice spacing stays the same.

120
00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:57.779
Simon N Sponberg: So this explains why they have the same steady-state properties, because when you activate these to an isometric condition.

121
00:27:57.860 --> 00:27:59.770
Simon N Sponberg: They have the same lattice spacing.

122
00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:17.079
Simon N Sponberg: But if you're gonna go back and forth between contracting and relaxing, the lattice spacing on the muscle on the left is gonna change, and the lattice spacing on the muscle on the right is not gonna change. And Travis was able to show, then, that what's actually happening is the muscle on the left, during a work loop.

123
00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:26.240
Simon N Sponberg: The mean change in the lattice spacing is actually undergoing a larger excursion in the activated muscle on the left than on the right.

124
00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:33.760
Simon N Sponberg: And it's undergoing a 1 nanometer larger change in lattice spacing than the muscle on the right.

125
00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:44.369
Simon N Sponberg: And that change in lattice spacing occurs right at the time when that muscle would need to produce larger amounts of force in order to act like a motor.

126
00:28:45.000 --> 00:29:07.479
Simon N Sponberg: And, so what the basic story here is that one of these is already constrained, and then expands out, and that allows it to do more work, and one is already at its extended point, and produces less binding, and does net negative work because of it. And Travis actually did a bunch of spatially explicit modeling about this using some of the models that we've talked about previously that Bert Tanner talked about.

127
00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:13.650
Simon N Sponberg: I'll skip through this and just say we fit a bunch of parameters to that, and here's the…

